Note indeed!

Also note that you can do a lot better than 10 AC
levels; the limit is somewhere around 19 or 20, but
after 9, it can take multiple tries for each
additional improvement, and with each additional
improvement, the odds of the next tries working are
worse.

This process therefore sucks up a _lot_ of money,
expect to bribe the priest _dozens_ of times, but if
you

    1) bankrupt all shopkeepers for several levels
    before hitting mines at all: the credit cloning
    scam is nice here, having the luck to ID a lot
    of gems is cool too, at least for some kinds of
    shops, but murdering shopkeepers is also
    successful, you just lose your friends in the
    price ID game if you choose that route

    2) clean out all vaults, possibly using the next
    trick

    3) dip down to just toplevel mines, get a
    pickaxe, go back up, and go tunneling through
    several regular dungeon levels looking for
    random loot or unannounced vaults (nothing says
    you cannot tunnel the rocky parts of Mines on
    your way to Mines Town, ore even below Mine
    Town, too)

    4) bankrupt _all_ of the shopkeepers in Mines
    Town (Izchak can take a really, really long
    time, because usually what he sells isn't very
    pricy, but he seems (may be FUD on my part) to
    always start with a generously full money pouch,
    so persist) before visiting the temple or
    between visits

you should have lots of zorkmids for priest bribing.

Stay at XL 1 until _after_ you have glommed all the
protection you ever want to "buy", and this is much,
much cheaper, since every XL level adds to the cost
multiplier; with practice, that isn't very hard to
do at all. Also, this keeps the randomly generated
monsters (almost always) within the range your pets
can handle.

To make all this work, you will really be helped by:

    a) one, or better several large pets to keep the
    monsters off your back so the pets gain the XPs
    (and buff up and get even better at helping you)
    and you don't get stuck killing monsters

    b) a ring of slow digestion or a really good
    relationship with your deity on the subject of
    whining for food, or a visit to Sokoban along
    the way to heavy up on food, so you don't have
    to compete with your pets for corpses

    c) a good hardware-derived armor class so you
    don't have to hit back at monsters, but can
    survive long enough to let your pets arrive to
    rescue you; pets can help you determine that
    dropped armor is safe to test-wear, the top
    couple levels of Mines are just _rife_ with low
    level monsters in good armor, mostly dwarfs,
    who, if you are patient and test-wear
    everything, will kit you out nicely in stuff you
    can toss later (when you shift from wimp-mode to
    god-spellcaster-mode _after_ you are protected
    to the max and can afford to go around in wimpy
    armor again waiting for better armor or to
    enchant up what you have)

    d) wands, potions, and/or even spells (while
    your spellcast-worsening hardware AC is low)
    like sleep and teleport-away and confusion and
    paralysis, that don't kill monsters on your
    account, but give your pets good chances to do
    the chore instead

    e) (guess == possible FUD) a thoroughly dull
    daggar or something similar to carry that is
    worse at combat than bare hands, so accidental
    nudges don't harm monsters enough to kill them
    (but not if you're trying to keep "never hit
    with a wielded weapon" conduct, of course)

    f) a fair amount of good luck; this is trade-off
    of risk (and playing slowly and skillfully) in
    the short term against long term destructo-class
    wizardry

IMAO, only, of course, and I've never once ascended.

